course: dictionary
lesson: 12661
title: Dissipate: to fade away or spread out
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Dissipate is a formal word. It means to slowly fade, spread out, or disappear until nothing is left. Look at the clues in the sentence: «The fog dissipated as the sun came up.» Fog cannot be held or caught; it thins and vanishes. So dissipated must mean the fog faded away.
explain: The word can also describe something that makes another thing disappear. «The wind dissipated the smoke» means the wind spread the smoke out until it was gone. Ask yourself: what is disappearing, and what is causing it?
explain: In science, dissipate has a special meaning: to lose energy, usually as heat. «Energy is dissipated as heat» means some energy spreads out as heat and is lost from the machine. That is why machines are never 100% efficient. To check any meaning, swap in «faded away» or «spread out». If the sentence still makes sense, you have it.
fact: Dissipate means to slowly fade, spread out, or disappear until it is gone.
fact: «The fog dissipated» means the fog faded away.
fact: «The wind dissipated the smoke» means the wind spread the smoke out until it was gone.
fact: In science, «energy is dissipated as heat» means the energy spreads out as heat and is lost, so it is no longer useful to the machine.
fact: The noun is dissipation, and dissipate can also mean to waste money or energy foolishly.
exercise: «The fog dissipated as the sun came up.» What does dissipated mean here? | Grew thicker and darker | Was painted bright red | Turned into snow | *Faded away until gone
exercise: In science, «energy is dissipated as heat». What is happening to the energy? | It is stored in a battery forever | It turns into a loud song | It doubles in size | *It spreads out as heat and is lost from the machine
exercise: Which word in «The dissipation of the storm took all afternoon» is a noun form of dissipate? | storm | afternoon | *dissipation | took
test: «Anger dissipated when the boy took a deep breath.» What does dissipated suggest? | Anger was written down | Anger was thrown across the room | *Anger faded away and grew smaller | Anger got bigger and louder
test: «He dissipated all his savings on sweets in one week.» What does dissipated mean here? | Counted slowly and neatly | Saved carefully for a long time | *Wasted foolishly | Hid under the bed
exam: Which sentence uses dissipate correctly? | *The heat dissipated slowly from the warm stones | I dissipated a red apple | The fog dissipated by noon | Dissipate is a bright colour of paint
